    "content_md": "Ian Fike\nSan Francisco, CA · (206) 455-5217 · fike101@gmail.com · linkedin.com/in/ianfike\n\nAmbience Healthcare\nRe: Product Lead, Platform\n\nDear Ambience team,\n\nThe most formative stretch of my career was holding Livongo’s platform together while we\nexpanded from an initial offering into a multi-product portfolio. Protecting stability while rapidly\nexpanding shared infrastructure (registration, accounts, the data pipeline, product APIs, partner\nintegrations, the tools the support team lived in) was the foundation that allowed us to move\nfaster. Every new product gave reasons to fork. The work was knowing when to say no, when to\nrapidly build forward, paying down the migrations we had been deferring, and investing in the\nbusiness-critical infrastructure early enough to never limit our scale. Over my tenure, we had a\nnumber of wins: velocity rose 28%, enrollment rose 12%, support handle time fell 34%. All while\ngrowing 10x (30k covered lives to over 500k).\n\nWhat you’re hiring for is unusually ambitious. Most platforms that have to span multiple\nproducts, multiple care settings, and multiple EMR environments end up in one of three places:\nthey fragment into per-product offshoots, they harden into a foundation that slows every team\ndown, or they collapse under a monolith no one is brave enough to touch. Holding all three\nfailure modes off at once, while still moving fast enough to add new products and new health\nsystems, is the actual work of the role. It requires treating the platform as its own product,\nbuilding growth infrastructure that is invisible when it works, and shipping internal tooling and\nagents that quietly increase our speed as we grow. This is the work and complexity that drives\nme, and the kind of impact that actually makes complex spaces like healthcare better.\n\nPlatforms are design problems before they are engineering problems. The decisions that\ndetermine whether a foundation scales are made early, by design, and enforced later by code:\nwhat becomes a primitive and what stays a feature, what gets configured and what gets\nassumed, how the surface area is named and grouped, where the seams between products fall.\nThat’s the lens I bring. I came to product management through interaction design, owned\nLivongo’s design system before owning the platform that ran on top of it, and still build with the\nmaterials. I ship production systems on the side in TypeScript, Supabase Edge Functions, and\nPostgres, designing the APIs, identity flows, and feature-flag surface myself. I won’t claim parity\nwith a former full-time engineer in a system-design review. I will claim parity in deciding what\nbelongs in the system.\n\n\n\nIan Fike\n\f",
