    "content_md": "Ian Fike\nSan Francisco, CA · (206) 455-5217 · fike101@gmail.com · linkedin.com/in/ianfike\n\n\nStedi\nRe: Product Manager, Eligibility & Insurance Discovery\n\nDear Stedi team,\nThe most formative stretch of my career was holding Livongo’s platform together while we expanded\nfrom a single-condition product into a multi-condition portfolio. Protecting stability while rapidly\nexpanding shared infrastructure (registration, accounts, the data pipeline, partner integrations, the\npayer- and enterprise-facing surfaces) was the foundation that let us move faster. Every new product\ngave reasons to fork. The work was knowing when to say no, when to rapidly build forward, paying\ndown the migrations we had been deferring, and investing in the business-critical infrastructure early\nenough to never limit our scale. Over my tenure: velocity rose 28%, enrollment rose 12%, support\nhandle time fell 34%, and we grew 10x (30k covered lives to over 500k). All of it shipped in a HIPAA-\nregulated environment with clinicians, payers, and enterprise partners on the other end of every\nintegration.\nWhat you are building is the rare healthcare-software story I actually want to bet on. The clearinghouse\ncategory has been calcified for a generation, run by pre-internet incumbents charging modern prices for\nbatch FTP plumbing. A programmable clearinghouse with a real API surface, real developer experience,\nand real-time eligibility and benefit normalization is not an incremental improvement. It is the thing every\nhealthcare technology team I have worked with has wished existed and built workarounds for. Eligibility\n& Insurance Discovery is the right wedge: the workflow most providers and digital-health companies hit\nfirst, the data quality problem that compounds across every downstream transaction, and the place\nwhere speed, accuracy, and reliability either earn trust or burn it.\nEligibility was the engine of Livongo’s enrollment growth, and I owned the registration and marketing\nsurface that ran on top of it. Every member signup validated against an eligibility file from the payer or\nemployer, and every outreach campaign segmented against that same data to reach the population that\nwas covered but had not yet enrolled. I worked closely with data engineering on how eligibility records\nwere ingested, normalized, and exposed to the registration funnel and the growth systems. The\nfreshness and accuracy of that data was the difference between a two-minute signup and a frustrated\nsupport call, and the difference between a campaign that converted and one that wasted spend on the\nwrong audience. I have lived the eligibility-quality problem from the consumer side of the wire, and what\nStedi is building is the platform layer I always wished existed.\nOutside of advisor work, I read code daily and ship production systems on the side in TypeScript,\nSupabase, and Postgres. I design the APIs, identity flows, and feature-flag surface myself because I\nrefuse to be a technical PM who cannot contribute in a system-design review.\nI am in San Francisco and the remote-USA setup works. I would welcome the chance to walk through\nLivongo’s eligibility-engine decisions, how I think about API and data-quality tradeoffs, or whichever\neligibility problem is most in front of you right now.\n\nIan Fike\n\f",
